<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Meissen</h1><p class="st-meta">965 CE – 1423 CE · capital Meissen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Wettin march on the middle Elbe whose silver mines and rulers laid the foundations of the later Electorate and Kingdom of Saxony.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Margrave Gero</a> in 965, Otto I created a march around the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> in 929 among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sorbs">Sorbs</a>. A bishopric followed in 968. From 1089 the march was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettin</a> family, who kept it for more than eight centuries in its successor states.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettins</a> promoted German settlement among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sorbs">Sorbs</a> and founded towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>. The discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a> around 1168 made the margraves wealthy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-rich">Otto the Rich</a> used it to fortify his lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-margrave-of-meissen">Henry the Illustrious</a> added the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/landgraviate-of-thuringia">Landgraviate of Thuringia</a> in 1264, making the Wettins a leading princely house.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1423 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Sigismund</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Warlike</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-wittenberg">Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg</a> with its electoral dignity, after the Ascanian line died out. The margraviate merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>, and the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> moved up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> to the old lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-meissen.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Meissen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Tom Lemmens · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Generieke_vlag_van_Vlaanderen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>965 CE – 1423 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Meissen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Conrad the Great, Otto the Rich, Henry the Illustrious</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle High German, Sorbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>March of Meissen, Mark Meißen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1194&amp;focus=margraviate-of-meissen" aria-label="Margraviate of Meissen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4986 405 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Margraviate of Meissen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5306 661L5229 694M5306 661L5312 664M5306 661L5235 666"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5312 664h0M5235 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5306 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5306 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Meissen. <a href="/atlas?year=1194&amp;focus=margraviate-of-meissen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
